    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/graph-management/graph-rendering.md#provenance-1",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Graph rendering: one set of rules for every drawn graph",
      "prov:value": "This repository draws graphs in at least six places, and until this skill each\none followed its own conventions. Owner, 2026-09-23 (issue #1137): *\"make\nskill that applies to graphs in general.... then apply to papers, schemas,\nmath proofs/lean etc\"*.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "#1137",
        "2026-09-23"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-23"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/graph-management/graph-rendering.md#provenance-2",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "issue",
      "cat-harness:section": "The ten rules",
      "prov:value": "Each rule was learned on the UML overview (issue #990). The failure each one\nprevents is stated beside it.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "#990"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/graph-management/graph-rendering.md#provenance-3",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "The ten rules",
      "prov:value": "1. **Draw from a declared graph, never by hand.** Every node and edge in the\n   picture is read from a source: a schema, a declaration, a BPMN file, a\n   `uses[]` list, a dependency cache. A wrong picture is fixed at its\n   source. A hand-edited diagram is overwritten on the next run, and a check\n   fails while it is stale.\n2. **Groups are declared sub-graphs.** A box around nodes is a named\n   sub-graph (a `directories[]` entry, a chapter, a package), never a\n   grouping the renderer invented. Owner, 2026-09-23: *\"sections are\n   sub-graphs in a harness\"*.\n3. **No empty box without a reason.** A node drawn with nothing in it reads\n   as a rendering fault; the owner asked *\"why empty?\"* of exactly that. A\n   node with no fields to show carries one line saying why. A node whose\n   shape could not be determined is drawn dashed, as a finding, never as a\n   quiet empty shape. The UML overview went from 242 empty boxes to 0.\n4. **Edge kinds are drawn distinctly and never merged.** A graph has more\n   than one kind of edge ([`edge-kinds-and-blast-radius`](edge-kinds-and-blast-radius.md)).\n   Editorial `uses[]` and a content type's formal edges, enforced and prose-only\n   references, and a field-carried link versus one declared elsewhere (the\n   UML's dashed `bootstrap.processes:skill` edge) each get their own line style and a\n   legend. Merging two kinds into one line destroys the signal every metric\n   on that graph is computed from.\n5. **Pick the layout engine by what the graph is, and write down why.**\n   See §\"Layout engines\" below. The choice is a claim with a measurement\n   behind it, not a taste.\n6. **Offer two orientations from one source.** A tall graph on a wide screen\n   and a wide graph on a phone are both unreadable. Derive the second view\n   from the first (`landscapeOf` in `scripts/plantuml-render.ts`) rather than keeping\n   two sources that can drift apart. Owner: *\"can we have portrait and\n   landscape views?\"*\n7. **Declare colour once.** Colour a node by its family (for the UML: schema,\n   scenario, process, state, test) in one stylesheet, and have every renderer\n   read that stylesheet (`uml-palette.ts`). A second palette is a second answer\n   free to disagree with the first.\n8. **Stamp the output with its source's hash.** A rendering whose staleness\n   check needs the renderer is a check CI cannot run: Java, a browser, or a\n   font set that measures text a pixel differently. Write the sha256 of the\n   exact source text into the output and compare stamps. The UML and\n   block-graph SVGs do this through `scripts/plantuml-render.ts`, which every\n   PlantUML generator shares, with its pinned jar and one JVM for all diagrams.\n9. **Show it in a zoomable figure.** On the site, use the `bpmn-figure`\n   markup so `docs-ui.js` adds zoom, reset and full-width controls, and the\n   export ones: download the SVG, download a 2× PNG, or copy it (as an image\n   where the browser allows, else as SVG text, and it says which; #1270). A pages\n   template missing `layout: default` renders with no site script and so no\n   controls, and nothing reports it.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "#1270",
        "2026-09-23"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-23"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/graph-management/graph-rendering.md#provenance-4",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "The ten rules",
      "prov:value": "   **There is ONE viewer, and every diagram on a folio or IG page gets it.**\n   It is `mountFigure` in `docs-ui.js`; `mountFigures` decides what is a\n   figure. Do not write a second one for a new kind of page. Extend what\n   `mountFigures` and `inlineDiagrams` recognise instead. Since bean `n7f8`\n   (owner, 2026-10-06, on an IG's sequence-diagrams page: *\"…are now\n   resiable/scrollable like the bpmn diagrams are. tehy should be\"*), it\n   takes each way a page embeds a drawing:",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean n7f8",
        "2026-10-06"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-06"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/graph-management/graph-rendering.md#provenance-5",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Layout engines",
      "prov:value": "Measured on the UML overview, 2026-09-23. Re-measure before relying on it for\na different graph.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-23"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-23"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/kg/graph-management/graph-rendering.md#provenance-6",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Layout engines",
      "prov:value": "| engine | use it for | what it gets wrong |\n|---|---|---|\n| **stored layout** (BPMN DI) | a graph whose author placed the nodes | nothing to choose: never re-lay a graph that carries its own layout |\n| **ELK** (`!pragma layout elk` in PlantUML) | the compact, portrait default; it routes edges around boxes | it lays everything top to bottom and **ignores `left to right direction` and arrow hints** (output byte-identical). Unconnected groups end up in one wide row. |\n| **ELK + hidden links** | a grid of unconnected groups (an overview) | nothing, if the column count suits the orientation: `ceil(√(n/2))` for portrait, `ceil(√(2n))` for landscape |\n| **Graphviz dot, left to right, polyline edges** | the landscape view of a connected graph | it can route a long edge across a box, which is why ELK stays the portrait default. Use `nodesep 70`, `ranksep 160`. |\n| Graphviz with **orthogonal** edges | avoid | it places edge labels away from their edges and runs lines through boxes. The owner: *\"make wider, its messy\"*. |\n| Mermaid (dagre) | a secondary view where CSS classes on nodes matter | the same model came out three times taller than ELK's |\n| **Graphviz dot in the browser** (WASM, `unflatten` then `dot`) | the interactive view a reader rearranges by dragging nodes ([`kg-subgraph-layout`](kg-subgraph-layout.md)) | like ELK it sets unconnected clusters, and unconnected nodes in one cluster, on one rank: fold both into grids with invisible, marked anchors |"
    }
  ]
}
